-- title:
--   η-branch: both Drinfeld lattices equal p⁻¹ diag(p,1) ℤₚ²
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and $\iota\colon \mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2}) \to \mathbb{W}(k)$ a ring homomorphism, where `Zp2 p` denotes $\mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and `pIdeal p B` the ideal generated by $p$. Write $\bar\jmath$ for the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map $\mathbb{W}(k) \to \mathbb{W}(k)/p$.
--
--   **The formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ and its rigidification.** $\Phi$ is an object of `FormalODModule p (WittVector p k ⧸ pIdeal p (WittVector p k))`: a commutative two-dimensional formal group law $F$ over $\mathbb{W}(k)/p$, an additive and multiplicative action `act` of $\mathbb{W}(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ by law endomorphisms with $\mathrm{act}(1) = \mathrm{id}$, and a law endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi \circ \varpi = \mathrm{act}(p)$ and $\varpi \circ \mathrm{act}(a) = \mathrm{act}(\sigma a) \circ \varpi$. The hypotheses on $\Phi$ are: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\jmath$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{Lie}_0$ and $\mathrm{Lie}_1$ (the intersections of the kernels of $\mathrm{lieAct}(a) - \bar\jmath(a)$, respectively of its $p$-th power twist) are complementary in $\mathrm{Lie}(\Phi)$ and each invertible; `hΦ4`, that $\mathrm{act}(p)$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that $\mathrm{Lie}_0 \subseteq \ker(\mathrm{lieVarpi})$, where $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$ is multiplication by the linear part of $\varpi$; and `hcΦ`, that the graded pieces of degrees $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $F$ — the $f$ with $\mathrm{endAct}(\mathrm{actEnd}([c]))f = \mathrm{homothety}(\bar\jmath([c])^{p^n})f$ for all $c \in \mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ — are complementary. Let $D_\Phi$ be the graded Cartier module datum `Φ.toGradedCartierModuleData` $\bar\jmath$ `hcΦ` (module the Cartier module of $F$, with Frobenius, integral Verschiebung, the action of $\varpi$, and the two graded submodules as pieces). Further data: an additive map $r_\Phi \colon \mathbb{Z}_p^2 \to \mathrm{NMod}(D_\Phi)$, and the hypothesis `hrΦ` that for every canonical $L$-map $L$ on $D_\Phi$ the map $r_\Phi$ is a bijection of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ onto $\mathrm{etaPiece}\,L\,0 = \eta(L) \cap \mathrm{nPiece}\,0$.
--
--   **The matrix.** $g \in \mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$, with `hg` : $g = \mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
--
--   **The edge family.** $E :=$ `EdgeFamily.edgeRingCharP p (WittVector p k ⧸ pIdeal p (WittVector p k))`, the chart ring `chartERing` of $\mathbb{W}(k)/p$ at $0$, with its two elements $\xi, \eta$; $X$ is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module over $E$ and $\gamma \colon \mathrm{Fin}\,2 \to \mathrm{Cart}(X.F)$. The hypotheses are: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\iota$ followed by $\mathbb{W}(k) \to \mathbb{W}(k)/p \to E$, i.e. $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$ and the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_l)$ has unit determinant; `hγa`, that $\gamma$ has structure constants `EdgeFamily.edgeRingConstants p` (the edge constants formed from $\xi$ and $\eta$), meaning that for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi \cdot \gamma_i$ is the sum $\sum_{m<N} V^m(\mathrm{homothety}(a_{m,i})\gamma_{\mathrm{piIndex}(m,i)})$ plus $V^N h$ for some $h$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special for that structure map; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   **The node point and the node isogeny.** $f_0 \colon E \to \mathbb{W}(k)/p$ with `hf₀` : $f_0$ splits the structural map $\mathbb{W}(k)/p \to E$, and `hf₀ξ`, `hf₀η` : $f_0(\xi) = f_0(\eta) = 0$. Next, $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and a series $\rho_0$ over $\mathbb{W}(k)/p$ with `hρ₀` : $\rho_0$ is an isogeny of height $4m$ from $\Phi$ to $X.\mathrm{map}\,f_0$ (an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$). Let $t_0 :=$ `Rigidified.mk` $(X.\mathrm{map}\,f_0)\,m\,\bar\rho_0$ be the rigidified triple over $\mathbb{W}(k)/p$ obtained by reducing $\rho_0$ modulo $p$. The hypotheses attached to $t_0$ are: `hOD₀`, that the series of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar{X}_{t_0}$; `hcb`, that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of $\bar{X}_{t_0}$ base-changed along the identity of $\mathbb{W}(k)/p$ are complementary; and `hcΦg`, the corresponding complementarity for $\bar\Phi$ base-changed along the identity. Finally, $a \in \mathbb{N}$ and the normalisation `hN`: for each $i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,2$,
--   $$p^a \cdot \mathrm{rigidNum}(\iota, \mathrm{hc}\Phi, r_\Phi, \dots)(e_i) = p^{a+m} \cdot v_i,$$
--   where $e_i$ is the $i$-th standard vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, $\mathrm{rigidNum}$ is the composite of the $n$-maps induced by the base change $\bar\Phi \to \bar\Phi_S$ and by the reduction of the rigidifying isogeny with $r_\Phi$, and $v_0 = \mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0, 0)$, $v_1 = \mathrm{nVarpi}(\mathrm{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1, 0))$, with $\bar\gamma_i$ the image of $\gamma_i$ under base change along $f_0$, then modulo $p$, then along the identity.
--
--   **The edge isogeny.** $\rho_1$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $(X.\mathrm{map}\,f_0)$ pushed to $E$ into $X$, subject to: `hρ₁0`, that the induced map on Cartier modules sends the image of $\gamma_0$ to $p\,\gamma_0 - V([\eta^{p^2}]\cdot\gamma_1)$; `hρ₁1`, that it sends the image of $\gamma_1$ to $p\,\gamma_1 - V([\xi^{p^2}]\cdot\gamma_0)$, where $V$ is the integral Verschiebung and $[\cdot]$ the Teichmüller lift; and `hρ₁h`, that $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   **The geometric point and the quadruple.** $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, $y \colon E \to \Omega$ a ring homomorphism with `hyξ` : $y(\xi) = 0$ and `hyη` : $y(\eta) \neq 0$. $Q$ is a Drinfeld datum over $\Omega$ for $\pi = p$: two maps $N_0, N_1$ from $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$ to full $\mathbb{Z}_p$-lattices in $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ with $N_0(x) \le N_1(x)$ and $p\,N_1(x) \subseteq N_0(x)$, the membership sets being open, together with invertible $\Omega$-modules $T_0, T_1$, maps $\Pi_0, \Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$ in both orders, and comparison maps $u_0, u_1$ from the base-changed lattices to the stalks. The hypothesis `hQ` states that the pushed rigidified triple `Rigidified.mk` $X\,(m+1)$ $(\bar\rho_1 \circ \bar\rho_0)$ over $E$ — with $\rho_0$ pushed to $E$ and reduced, followed by the reduction of $\rho_1$ — transported along $y$ satisfies `IsCartierQuadruple` with respect to $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$, the structure map $y \circ (\mathbb{W}(k) \to \mathbb{W}(k)/p \to E)$ and $Q$; among the clauses of that predicate are that the triple's series is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism $\bar\Phi \to \bar{X}$, that $T_0, T_1$ are $\Omega$-linearly identified with the two Lie pieces of $X$ compatibly with $\Pi_0$, $\Pi_1$ and $\mathrm{lieVarpi}$, and that at every prime the lattices $N_0$, $N_1$ consist exactly of the vectors realised by $\eta$-sections of the associated graded Cartier data over suitable localisations, compatibly with $u_0$ and $u_1$. Finally $x$ is a point of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Both lattices at $x$ coincide with the underlying $\mathbb{Z}_p$-submodule of $\mathbb{Q}_p^2$ obtained from the standard lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ by acting first with $g$ and then with the scalar matrix $p^{-1}$ (the inverse of the unit of $\mathbb{Q}_p$ given by the image of $p$, nonzero since $p$ is irreducible in $\mathbb{Z}_p$). That is, $Q.N_0(x)$ equals that submodule, and $Q.N_1(x)$ equals the same submodule; with `hg` this lattice is $p^{-1}\,\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\,\mathbb{Z}_p^2 = \mathbb{Z}_p e_0 \oplus p^{-1}\mathbb{Z}_p e_1$. In particular $Q.N_0(x) = Q.N_1(x)$.
--
--   This is the lattice half of the computation of the Drinfeld datum attached to a geometric point of the edge family of the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation lying on the branch $\xi = 0$, $\eta \neq 0$: there both lattices of any Cartier quadruple of the pushed rigidified triple are the single vertex $p^{-1}\,\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\,\mathbb{Z}_p^2$, so that the corresponding point of the formal upper half plane is the odd vertex. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_eq_zero_of_apply_eta_ne_zero`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.exists_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_edge_isogeny_of_apply_xi_eq_zero_of_apply_eta_ne_zero), which assembles the lattice and line data at such a point.
